date with Lee and like someone had taped it and you could watch yourselves on that first

508
00:21:03.430 --> 00:21:05.690
date, wouldn't you just like lose it?

509
00:21:05.850 --> 00:21:07.850
And I was like, oh my God, I would lose it.

510
00:21:07.950 --> 00:21:13.250
And you're like, what if Jim asked the documentary crew to put together the footage and he shows

511
00:21:13.250 --> 00:21:13.890
it to Pam?

512
00:21:14.250 --> 00:21:16.890
And I was like, oh my God, I would be a puddle.

513
00:21:17.030 --> 00:21:20.070
And then we just all got so excited about this idea.

514
00:21:20.750 --> 00:21:22.190
That sounds like a blunt idea.

515
00:21:22.410 --> 00:21:23.950
That sounds like blunt genius right there.

516
00:21:24.050 --> 00:21:24.850
And I just stole it.

517
00:21:25.230 --> 00:21:25.830
I just stole it.

518
00:21:25.830 --> 00:21:26.290
Well, that was the thing.

519
00:21:26.430 --> 00:21:30.790
You were like, Emily was like, what's the thing that this couple has that no other couple

520
00:21:30.790 --> 00:21:31.110
has?

521
00:21:31.170 --> 00:21:31.890
So smart.

522
00:21:32.130 --> 00:21:32.790
Oh my God.

523
00:21:33.010 --> 00:21:33.970
It's so smart.

524
00:21:34.150 --> 00:21:34.850
It was so smart.

525
00:21:34.930 --> 00:21:39.450
By the way, I remember when they were putting that together, I was so excited to see it

526
00:21:39.450 --> 00:21:43.270
too because we didn't know what they were going to put together in that whole situation.

527
00:21:43.350 --> 00:21:44.770
Oh God, it was so much.

528
00:21:44.890 --> 00:21:45.850
It was so much.

529
00:21:46.030 --> 00:21:47.330
What episode is that?

530
00:21:47.330 --> 00:21:48.290
I don't know.

531
00:21:48.450 --> 00:21:49.170
We're not there yet.

532
00:21:49.170 --> 00:21:51.110
It's very close to the end though.

533
00:21:51.470 --> 00:21:55.370
I mean, it might be like even the one of the two before the finale.

534
00:21:55.750 --> 00:22:02.270
Guys, do you remember, again, maybe I'm remembering wrong because I'm 98 years old, but do you

535
00:22:02.270 --> 00:22:08.490
remember that like we, Greg hadn't talked to Steve like officially about the end?

536
00:22:08.690 --> 00:22:09.250
Do you remember this?

537
00:22:09.550 --> 00:22:09.750
Yeah.

538
00:22:10.010 --> 00:22:12.290
Like he had thrown it out to Steve in the beginning.

539
00:22:12.410 --> 00:22:13.410
Am I remembering this right?

540
00:22:13.750 --> 00:22:18.670
And then like, as we got closer to the end, it was like, so is Steve going to, and I remember

541
00:22:18.670 --> 00:22:19.510
he was shooting something.

542
00:22:19.590 --> 00:22:20.910
It wasn't like he didn't want to do it.

543
00:22:20.970 --> 00:22:22.550
It was just like scheduling and all of that.

544
00:22:22.670 --> 00:22:26.390
And then, but Greg, because he's so nice, wasn't pressuring anyone.

545
00:22:26.390 --> 00:22:30.310
And then that the whole Steve reveal was kind of a big reveal for us too.

546
00:22:30.470 --> 00:22:32.070
Like his appearance was huge.

547
00:22:32.390 --> 00:22:32.790
Yeah.

548
00:22:32.950 --> 00:22:36.330
He told us like very close to when it was going to happen.

549
00:22:36.470 --> 00:22:39.090
He, I think he got you, me and Rain aside, John.

550
00:22:39.330 --> 00:22:43.750
And he told us a couple days before, but then he swore us to secrecy.

551
00:22:44.050 --> 00:22:48.570
And Jenna told me that Rain pulled her aside and said, Jenna, you cannot tell Angela.

552
00:22:49.130 --> 00:22:50.830
Like, I was so scared.

553
00:22:52.350 --> 00:22:53.250
I didn't know.

554
00:22:53.410 --> 00:22:58.490
The first time I saw Steve, I was walking to hair and makeup and I forget who I was walking

555
00:22:58.490 --> 00:23:00.230
with, but they were like, you know, Steve is here.

556
00:23:00.370 --> 00:23:01.490
And I was like, shut up.

557
00:23:01.770 --> 00:23:02.070
Whatever.

558
00:23:02.390 --> 00:23:03.170
What were you talking about?

559
00:23:03.290 --> 00:23:04.370
You didn't know until the day?

560
00:23:04.650 --> 00:23:09.130
I opened the door and he was in the chair and he turned around and he goes, and, and

561
00:23:09.130 --> 00:23:11.770
I was like, ah, I was like, what's happening?

562
00:23:13.270 --> 00:23:15.670
That rain scene where I say I can't do it.

563
00:23:15.850 --> 00:23:16.430
Oh my God.

564
00:23:16.570 --> 00:23:17.930
That was so well done.

565
00:23:17.930 --> 00:23:18.250
Yeah.

566
00:23:18.250 --> 00:23:18.910
So smart.

567
00:23:19.070 --> 00:23:19.650
So great.

568
00:23:19.950 --> 00:23:20.530
So smart.

569
00:23:20.650 --> 00:23:23.010
And when I say well done, I mean, I had nothing to do with it.

570
00:23:23.090 --> 00:23:27.330
Like how great that we were on a show that the writing was so good that it was like, it

571
00:23:27.330 --> 00:23:28.270
was like Christmas morning.

572
00:23:28.410 --> 00:23:29.570
Every day you read a script.

573
00:23:29.670 --> 00:23:34.470
You were like, oh, we just got to show up and play the beSt.

574
00:23:34.710 --> 00:23:34.890
Yeah.

575
00:23:35.450 --> 00:23:40.370
Well, that leads me to this question, John, if you could go back and relive a moment from

576
00:23:40.370 --> 00:23:42.230
your time on the office, what would it be?

577
00:23:44.010 --> 00:23:44.730
Oh man.

578
00:23:45.910 --> 00:23:47.270
It wouldn't be one moment.

579
00:23:47.270 --> 00:23:48.570
It would be a routine.

580
00:23:48.910 --> 00:23:54.170
It would be, I loved so much coming in every morning and seeing the entirety of the crew

581
00:23:54.170 --> 00:23:58.130
and cast that we would come on and we hadn't really started the day when we were catching

582
00:23:58.130 --> 00:24:00.130
up over the week about what happened over the weekend.

583
00:24:00.130 --> 00:24:04.290
And every single crew member was our family.

584
00:24:04.510 --> 00:24:06.510
Every single cast member was our family.

585
00:24:06.910 --> 00:24:12.390
And I genuinely mean it when I say I miss it as much as I miss, you know, real family

586
00:24:12.390 --> 00:24:14.250
stuff and real, real friends stuff.

587
00:24:14.250 --> 00:24:17.650
I mean, it's, it's, it's, it, I don't know.

588
00:24:17.890 --> 00:24:18.630
I don't know.

589
00:24:18.770 --> 00:24:19.970
Maybe a lot of people have this.

590
00:24:20.090 --> 00:24:26.310
I don't know if people get to have and create de facto families separate from their own,

591
00:24:26.410 --> 00:24:28.110
separate from their own realities.

592
00:24:28.110 --> 00:24:28.730
You know what I mean?

593
00:24:28.770 --> 00:24:31.490
That, that at work or whatever, but it wasn't just work.

594
00:24:31.490 --> 00:24:35.330
It was like, we became this fake real family.

595
00:24:35.330 --> 00:24:35.890
Yeah.

596
00:24:36.170 --> 00:24:40.290
And, uh, I, it was, it was really those in between moments, those, those catching up

597
00:24:40.290 --> 00:24:41.610
moments were just the beSt.

598
00:24:41.710 --> 00:24:47.050
And, you know, from talking to you guys and hearing about, you know, new relationships

599
00:24:47.050 --> 00:24:51.570
or, oh my God, I think I'm pregnant and all that stuff, but also like crew members and

600
00:24:51.570 --> 00:24:56.930
laughing and Steve coming for a couple of days, you know, he might've been shooting

601
00:24:56.930 --> 00:24:57.410
something else.

602
00:24:57.570 --> 00:25:01.890
Those one couple of times, then he'd tell me a bit that he was doing and, or when he pretended

603
00:25:01.890 --> 00:25:05.990
to fake vomit and that was my favorite bit I've ever seen in my life ever.

604
00:25:06.530 --> 00:25:07.630
Like just little stuff like that.

605
00:25:07.750 --> 00:25:11.310
It was always the in between moments for me that I, I think I remember the moSt.

606
00:25:11.490 --> 00:25:14.170
And certainly only because you're on the last season.

607
00:25:14.310 --> 00:25:21.910
So I can say the, the evil genius of Greg Daniels saying, we're going to shoot a scene

608
00:25:21.910 --> 00:25:24.050
where we're all going to poor Richards at the end.

609
00:25:24.190 --> 00:25:27.810
And we went out and we all hook line and sinker that one.

610
00:25:27.890 --> 00:25:30.190
And we all went out and we're like, oh yeah, of course we're going to poor Richard.

611
00:25:30.290 --> 00:25:30.550
No problem.

612
00:25:30.550 --> 00:25:33.670
And the whole cast kept going in and out of the door like four times.

613
00:25:34.250 --> 00:25:38.370
And so we were out in the lobby waiting for him to call reset and we were laughing and

614
00:25:38.370 --> 00:25:38.670
joking.

615
00:25:39.130 --> 00:25:43.390
And when we came back in, I don't know how he did it, but he assembled the entirety of

616
00:25:43.390 --> 00:25:46.270
the crew and we can't, I'm like choking up just talking about it.

617
00:25:46.330 --> 00:25:51.150
But then when you come back in and you see like your TV dad and all of your family waiting

618
00:25:51.150 --> 00:25:53.250
there and clapping, and he said, that's the end of the office.

619
00:25:53.390 --> 00:25:58.350
And I, I think I black, I like, I think I like the color black came out of my mouth.

620
00:25:58.350 --> 00:26:04.570
Like I, it was just the scariest, most beautiful, horrific moment of my life.

621
00:26:04.730 --> 00:26:08.230
And then 30 minutes later, I took the Dunder Mifflin sign, which I think I've always lied

622
00:26:08.230 --> 00:26:09.990
to Greg that I didn't take it, but I did.

623
00:26:10.810 --> 00:26:16.650
It's one of my favorite memories of our last day was watching you try to put it in the back

624
00:26:16.650 --> 00:26:17.250
of your car.

625
00:26:17.970 --> 00:26:23.130
I was walking through the parking lot and I was like, oh, it's a Dunder Mifflin sign.

626
00:26:23.830 --> 00:26:24.170
Yeah.

627
00:26:24.270 --> 00:26:25.450
And I was like, don't look over here.

628
00:26:26.030 --> 00:26:27.070
Nothing's happening here.

629
00:26:27.190 --> 00:26:28.670
It wasn't quite dark enough.

630
00:26:28.790 --> 00:26:31.170
So it just looked like I was putting a body in a car.

631
00:26:31.310 --> 00:26:37.610
And then I remember Greg came up to me at the end party and was like, Hey, um, we've

632
00:26:37.610 --> 00:26:38.370
been looking for the sign.

633
00:26:38.490 --> 00:26:39.190
Do you know where it is?

634
00:26:39.270 --> 00:26:41.330
And I, it was literally like confessing to my dad.

635
00:26:41.330 --> 00:26:44.250
And I went, no, nope, no.

636
00:26:44.450 --> 00:26:46.150
And he goes, oh, that was the one thing I wanted.

637
00:26:46.250 --> 00:26:48.190
And I was like, oh boy.

638
00:26:48.510 --> 00:26:49.350
Oh God.

639
00:26:51.210 --> 00:26:53.970
I never told anyone I saw you putting it in the car.

640
00:26:54.370 --> 00:26:55.190
Well, now you did.

641
00:26:55.390 --> 00:26:56.170
Now you told everyone.

642
00:26:56.950 --> 00:26:58.650
And I was dressed like a cat burglar.

643
00:27:00.570 --> 00:27:05.670
John, it's really funny to me because the moment that you would most wish to relive, just

644
00:27:05.670 --> 00:27:08.030
the moment of talking to the cast and crew.

645
00:27:08.030 --> 00:27:09.250
I know what you're going to say, Jenna.

646
00:27:09.530 --> 00:27:10.770
I know what you're going to say.

647
00:27:11.230 --> 00:27:12.470
Is Rain's nightmare.

648
00:27:13.090 --> 00:27:13.310
What?

649
00:27:13.710 --> 00:27:19.190
Do you remember when we were all sitting on set once and it was like a Tuesday and all

650
00:27:19.190 --> 00:27:24.330
of a sudden Rain out of nowhere, he bursts out and he goes, attention, attention.

651
00:27:24.330 --> 00:27:25.530
I need everyone's attention.

652
00:27:25.690 --> 00:27:26.230
Listen to me.

653
00:27:26.590 --> 00:27:31.110
From now on, we only ask, how was your weekend on Mondays?

654
00:27:31.410 --> 00:27:36.330
You get one day, you get one day to ask me, how are you and how was your weekend?

655
00:27:36.470 --> 00:27:37.190
And then it's enough.

656
00:27:37.370 --> 00:27:38.550
I can't take it anymore.

657
00:27:38.550 --> 00:27:39.770
He like fractured.

658
00:27:39.950 --> 00:27:41.030
I can't take the questions anymore.

659
00:27:41.410 --> 00:27:42.330
We were all like.

660
00:27:42.350 --> 00:27:44.310
It's like a Curb Your Enthusiasm episode.

661
00:27:44.410 --> 00:27:44.790
It was.

662
00:27:45.010 --> 00:27:45.250
What is happening?

663
00:27:45.470 --> 00:27:49.150
And I remember Dale was right by him and he was like, jeez.

664
00:27:50.170 --> 00:27:52.470
And Rain fully talks about this.

665
00:27:52.610 --> 00:27:54.010
We've had full conversations with him.

666
00:27:54.090 --> 00:27:58.330
And he's like, remember that day I told everyone, don't ask me about the weekend on Tuesdays?

667
00:27:58.410 --> 00:27:59.910
It just like short-circuited.

668
00:28:00.170 --> 00:28:00.220
Oh my God.

669
00:28:00.510 --> 00:28:02.290
Rain would not want to revisit that.

670
00:28:02.410 --> 00:28:03.090
He would not.

671
00:28:03.090 --> 00:28:12.130
By the way, you know, another thing that you guys were both involved in was, I think, yeah, I think I said to Greg, do you mind if I make some sort of documentary about the end?

672
00:28:12.310 --> 00:28:13.670
And my buddy Danny shot it.

673
00:28:13.830 --> 00:28:17.950
And I remember he showed me the rough cut of all of us saying goodbye to the office.

674
00:28:18.130 --> 00:28:22.510
And you guys all had the same reaction, which is, he was like, so what do you think about the end of the office?

675
00:28:22.510 --> 00:28:31.370
And you guys, both of you went, the end of the office, wow, it was just, we all couldn't even get past the first question.

676
00:28:31.550 --> 00:28:32.210
It was too much.

677
00:28:32.390 --> 00:28:34.430
It was a beautiful, beautiful time.

678
00:28:34.450 --> 00:28:35.490
It really was.

679
00:28:35.990 --> 00:28:36.890
It really was.

680
00:28:36.970 --> 00:28:39.270
And you guys will have to live through that again, right?

681
00:28:39.570 --> 00:28:40.250
Pretty soon.

682
00:28:40.250 --> 00:28:41.370
In about 14 weeks or whatever.

683
00:28:41.510 --> 00:28:41.670
Yeah.

684
00:28:42.430 --> 00:28:44.490
And you guys, are you going to run it back?

685
00:28:44.610 --> 00:28:47.450
Are you just going to, like, people watch the show over and over?

686
00:28:47.450 --> 00:28:50.350
Why wouldn't you just do this again and find new things in episodes?

687
00:28:50.510 --> 00:28:52.810
People are writing in and saying this to us, John.

688
00:28:53.090 --> 00:28:54.090
They're just like, do it again.

689
00:28:54.250 --> 00:28:54.630
I would.

690
00:28:55.490 --> 00:28:56.270
Do it again.

691
00:28:56.310 --> 00:28:57.630
Let's talk about it in a different way.

692
00:28:58.230 --> 00:29:01.150
I mean, clearly Presentsky will tell the same story, so who cares?

693
00:29:02.850 --> 00:29:04.110
This has been so much fun.

694
00:29:04.270 --> 00:29:04.830
So much fun.

695
00:29:04.850 --> 00:29:05.350
Is it over already?

696
00:29:05.510 --> 00:29:09.330
No, I'm going to bring us home with our call sheet questions that were on the back of the call sheet.

697
00:29:09.690 --> 00:29:10.850
God, I missed your accent.

698
00:29:11.230 --> 00:29:12.870
I'm going to bring it home with my lid.

699
00:29:13.810 --> 00:29:14.170
Sorry.

700
00:29:14.590 --> 00:29:17.030
Both of my sisters from Texas are here visiting.

701
00:29:18.110 --> 00:29:19.570
Oh, I can tell.

702
00:29:19.710 --> 00:29:20.390
Yes, I know.

703
00:29:20.510 --> 00:29:21.570
They've been here a few days.

704
00:29:22.370 --> 00:29:23.390
Here we go.

705
00:29:23.530 --> 00:29:24.810
The call sheet questions.

706
00:29:25.510 --> 00:29:29.250
Number one, what was your first entertainment job?

707
00:29:30.430 --> 00:29:31.750
What does that entail?

708
00:29:31.890 --> 00:29:33.310
I think I was an extra.

709
00:29:34.970 --> 00:29:41.070
Well, actually, I remember because my first job was an extra in a insurance commercial

710
00:29:41.070 --> 00:29:46.710
that then they bumped me up to featured extra because I became the cashier.

711
00:29:47.450 --> 00:29:51.010
And the only reason why I remember that so vividly is because the woman who was checking out,

712
00:29:51.130 --> 00:29:56.230
who was the lead of the commercial, was Margo Martindale, who I later was in a movie called

713
00:29:56.230 --> 00:29:57.430
The Hollers, and she played my mom.

714
00:29:57.670 --> 00:29:59.090
Oh, my gosh.

715
00:29:59.770 --> 00:30:00.150
Yeah.

716
00:30:00.370 --> 00:30:02.890
And then they gave me my SAG card that day.

717
00:30:03.270 --> 00:30:06.370
And I remember her saying something to the effect of like, yeah, you better.

718
00:30:06.750 --> 00:30:07.290
That's right.

719
00:30:07.370 --> 00:30:09.610
You will give it to him because he's doing great.

720
00:30:09.790 --> 00:30:10.850
I think you're doing great.

721
00:30:10.850 --> 00:30:12.210
And I was like, oh, my God.

722
00:30:13.230 --> 00:30:17.030
Did you guys talk about that story when you did the movie together?

723
00:30:17.750 --> 00:30:18.490
Oh, yeah.

724
00:30:18.830 --> 00:30:21.670
I think she probably had something to do with getting me my SAG card.

725
00:30:21.870 --> 00:30:26.070
So, yeah, she is the most wonderful, wonderful.

726
00:30:26.430 --> 00:30:27.450
Have you guys worked with her?

727
00:30:27.670 --> 00:30:28.510
I have not.

728
00:30:28.770 --> 00:30:29.630
No, big fan.

729
00:30:29.890 --> 00:30:30.430
Big fan.

730
00:30:30.690 --> 00:30:32.430
Would probably not be cool around her.

731
00:30:32.510 --> 00:30:32.560
Get there quick.

732
00:30:32.770 --> 00:30:33.250
Would try.

733
00:30:34.490 --> 00:30:36.170
Okay, number two.

734
00:30:36.510 --> 00:30:38.550
Do you play a musical instrument?

735
00:30:39.090 --> 00:30:40.910
I did play a musical instrument.

736
00:30:41.090 --> 00:30:46.610
I played trumpet for nine years and probably could not do that at all right now.

737
00:30:46.810 --> 00:30:47.710
My lips would fall off.

738
00:30:47.850 --> 00:30:53.630
But I have been desperately trying to learn piano and guitar, and it's going on 15 years now.

739
00:30:53.750 --> 00:30:55.870
So I can play Let It Be.

740
00:30:55.870 --> 00:30:58.890
I remember you had a beautiful little upright piano, remember?

741
00:30:59.210 --> 00:30:59.430
That's right.

742
00:30:59.570 --> 00:31:01.050
And I felt like you played it really well.

743
00:31:01.050 --> 00:31:02.050
So maybe it's more than 15.

744
00:31:02.230 --> 00:31:02.750
I'm sorry.

745
00:31:04.170 --> 00:31:06.070
Can you play any song?

746
00:31:06.730 --> 00:31:09.670
Yes, I can play some Beatles stuff.

747
00:31:09.830 --> 00:31:12.790
I can play that amazing Stay.

748
00:31:13.570 --> 00:31:14.030
Brianna.

749
00:31:14.210 --> 00:31:15.210
Brianna sings Stay.

